= Data leakage (machine learning) =

== Overview ==
In machine learning, '''data leakage''' occurs when information that would not be legitimately available under the intended prediction or evaluation conditions improperly influences training, preprocessing, validation, model selection, or performance estimation.

Leakage can make a system appear more accurate than it will be in real use.

== Target leakage ==
Target leakage occurs when features contain information derived from or only known after the target outcome.

A model may then appear highly predictive while depending on information unavailable at prediction time.

== Train–test leakage ==
Leakage can occur when test information influences training through:

* duplicate or near-duplicate records;
* preprocessing fit before splitting;
* feature selection using all labels;
* shared individuals or groups across splits;
* temporal overlap;
* target-derived metadata;
* contamination of training data with test examples.

== Pipeline leakage ==
Any learned preprocessing step belongs inside the training pipeline.

For example, normalization statistics, imputation values, feature-selection criteria, or dimensionality-reduction components should normally be estimated using training data only.

== Security meaning ==
This article concerns predictive-model evaluation.

It is distinct from data leakage in computer security, where the issue is unauthorized disclosure or exfiltration.

== Benchmark contamination ==
[[Benchmark contamination]] is an evaluation-specific leakage problem in which benchmark information influences training or tuning.

It is important but narrower than machine-learning data leakage as a whole.

== Related concepts ==
[[Training set]], [[Validation set]], [[Test set]], [[Benchmark contamination]], [[Provenance]], [[Validation]], and [[Generalization (machine learning)]] define the relevant evaluation boundary.

